- `ERRORS.md:108` points at `VERIFICATION.md`, which **does not exist** in the repo.\n   - Row 19 (`ERRORS.md:70`) says \"Accepted for **all 2^32 patterns per argument**\" and `ERRORS.md:42` says \"the full 32-bit range is **swept randomly**\". The actual test `error_paths.rs:304-334` uses a fixed 76-element pattern list (76³ ≈ 4.4e5 calls) with no randomness. The claim is not supported by that row's assertions.\n   - `ERRORS.md:9-19` grep evidence — I re-ran all three greps and they reproduce exactly (exit 1 / lib.c:4 / lines 5-7 only). Accurate.\n   - Rows 1-18, 20, 21 each map to a real `#[test]` with real assertions; row 4's `20 + (bs+7)/8` and row 13's `f(1,2,0xFFFFFFFF)==20` are correct.\n\n7. **`CONFIGS.md` minor inaccuracies:** line 65 says seed `0x5EED_C0FFEE`, actual is `0x5EED_C0FF_EE00_0000` (`tests/common/mod.rs:366`). Rows 1-20 and 22-26 are all backed by real assertions; row 21 is the weak one (finding 3). `error_paths.rs:186` lists `0x1_0000_0000u64 as u32`, which is just `0` — mislabelled as an \"exactly-overflowing product\".\n\n8. **`symbol_parity.rs:70-76`** `is_runtime_glue` filters any name starting with `__`, which would hide a real C API symbol in the implementation-reserved namespace. Mitigated here by the `c_api.len() == 1` assertion at lines 108-112.\n\n9. `Cargo.toml:12` `panic = \"abort\"`: a Rust panic aborts the whole test binary rather than failing one test, so per-test attribution is lost (the run still fails — I confirmed with the panic mutant).\n\n## Gaps I would still want closed\n\n- **Assert the C `.so` is newer than `c_src/src/lib.c`** and **pin the C library filename** (`libtranslated_rust.so`, from the CMake project name) instead of `sorted().first()`.\n- Un-`#[ignore]` (or wire `--ignored` into `run_all_tests.sh`) the `exhaustive_axis` sweeps; ~9 min buys genuinely exhaustive coverage of `(P+7)/8` over all 2^32 `P`, of `18+ch` over all 2^32 `ch`, and of stereo `M` over all 2^32 `bd`.\n- **Residual unverifiable surface:** the only part of the 2^96 space not exhaustively covered by any test (mine included) is the truncating 32×32→32 multiply for *both* operands simultaneously large (`ch·bd` and `bs·M`, 2^64 pairs each). A needle mutant (`if bs==0xDEADBEEF && ch==0x12345678 && bd==0x0BADF00D { return 0 }`) survives the entire existing suite — I verified this. Only an SMT/bitvector equivalence proof would close it; no solver (z3/cvc5) is installed and there's no network. Structural argument in lieu: both sides emit the same `imul`/`shr` sequence over `Z/2^32`.\n- Replace `oracle()` with a derivation written from `lib.c` by a different route (e.g. `u64`-masked, as I did) so C+Rust can't share a misreading.\n\n**Nothing was modified.** `md5sum` of `src/lib.rs`, `tests/**`, `*.md`, `c_src/**` are unchanged (mtimes identical to the start of the audit); all mutation testing was done on a copy under `$TMPDIR/audit/mutrepo`. `cargo test` did write `target/` (`target/diff-so`, `target/{debug,release}`), which is unavoidable when running the suite."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a2d70ed94661af435 (use SendMessage with to: 'a2d70ed94661af435', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 153620\ntool_uses: 60\nduration_ms: 901964</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"25e5a781-4f1a-46b6-8172-dcad34d2c3fa","uuid":"c8b41f1a-330f-43f1-a7ca-73da8e90d8aa","timestamp":"2026-08-19T13:49:01.069Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are performing an INDEPENDENT ADVERSARIAL AUDIT of a C-to-Rust translation verification effort. Do NOT trust any claims in the existing markdown files — verify everything from primary sources yourself.\n\nWorking directory: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-pheWIC/translated_rust\n\nThe C ground truth is `c_src/src/lib.c` and `c_src/include/lib.h`. The Rust translation is `src/lib.rs`. The C shared library is already built at `c_src/build/libtranslated_rust.so`.\n\nIMPORTANT CONSTRAINTS:\n- You MUST NOT modify anything under `c_src/`.\n- You MUST NOT modify `src/lib.rs`, `tests/`, or any `.md` file. This is a READ-ONLY audit. Report findings only.\n- Use `$TMPDIR` for any scratch files. Use `cargo --offline` (there is no network).\n- Use timeouts on all commands (max 600s).\n\nYour job is to find any way the Rust could diverge from the C that the existing tests would MISS. Specifically:\n\n1. Read `c_src/src/lib.c` and `c_src/include/lib.h`. Independently derive the EXACT C semantics of `max_size_frame`, paying careful attention to:\n   - C integer promotion / usual arithmetic conversions on each subexpression (the operands mix `uint32_t` with `int` results of `!=` / `==` and the `int` literals `18U`, `7`, `8`, `2`, `32`).\n   - Whether any subexpression could be evaluated in `int` (32-bit signed) rather than `unsigned int`, which would make overflow UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR rather than wrapping. Check the odd `+ +7` (unary plus) too.\n   - Whether the division `/8` is signed or unsigned.\n   - Whether `unsigned int` is 32-bit on this platform (check that `uint32_t` == `unsigned int` so promotions behave as assumed).\n   State your conclusion clearly.\n\n2. Read `src/lib.rs` and determine whether it faithfully reproduces those semantics for ALL 2^96 inputs. Look for any input class where they could differ.\n\n3. Verify there is no untranslated C code: confirm the complete set of symbols the C `.so` exports, and that `src/lib.rs` covers all of them. Check for any other C/H files anywhere in the repo that CMake might compile.\n\n4. Independently write your OWN differential test (in $TMPDIR, e.g. a standalone C program or a Python `ctypes` script) that loads BOTH `c_src/build/libtranslated_rust.so` AND the Rust cdylib, and compares them on inputs YOU choose adversarially. Build the Rust cdylib yourself with:\n     cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-pheWIC/translated_rust && cargo build --offline --release --target-dir \"$TMPDIR/audit-target\"\n   The Rust .so will be at \"$TMPDIR/audit-target/release/libmax_size_frame_lib.so\".\n   Try hard to find a divergence. Include at minimum: all-zeros, all-0xFFFFFFFF, values that make each intermediate product hit exactly 2^32 and 2^32-1, channels in {0,1,2,3}, bitdepth in {0,31,32,33,0xFFFFFFFF}, and at least 1 million random triples.\n\n5. Review the existing test files `tests/common/mod.rs`, `tests/differential.rs`, `tests/error_paths.rs`, `tests/symbol_parity.rs`, `tests/exhaustive_axis.rs` and report:\n   - Any test that could pass VACUOUSLY (e.g. asserting nothing, comparing a value to itself, loading the same library twice, or loading a stale artifact).\n   - Whether the harness genuinely loads the Rust code via `libloading` (dlopen/dlsym) rather than calling it directly as a Rust function.\n   - Any row in `ERRORS.md` or `CONFIGS.md` that is claimed to be covered but is NOT actually covered by a real assertion.\n\nReport back concisely:\n- Your derived C semantics and whether any UB is possible.\n- PASS/FAIL for semantic equivalence, with any divergent input you found (exact values).\n- Any vacuous or misleading test.\n- Any gap in coverage you would still want closed.\nBe specific and cite file:line. Do not fix anything; just report.","agentId":"a2d70ed94661af435","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All background sweeps finished clean. Here is the audit.\n\n## 1. Derived C semantics (`c_src/src/lib.c:3-10`, `c_src/include/lib.h:1-5`)\n\n**Platform facts, verified with `_Generic` (not assumed):** `uint32_t` *is* `unsigned int` (`/usr/include/bits/types.h:42`: `typedef unsigned int __uint32_t`), `sizeof(int)==sizeof(unsigned)==4`, `_Generic((uint32_t)0, unsigned int: ...)` selects `unsigned int`.\n\n**Type of every subexpression (all confirmed by `_Generic` probes):**\n\n| subexpression | type | note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `channels != 2`, `channels == 2`, `bitdepth != 32` | `int` (0/1) | |\n| `channels * (channels != 2)` | **`unsigned int`** | equal rank ⇒ `int` operand converts to `unsigned` |\n| `blocksize * bitdepth`, and both further `*` | **`unsigned int`** | |\n| `bitdepth + (bitdepth != 32)` | **`unsigned int`** | `0xFFFFFFFF + 1 → 0`, defined wrap |\n| `+7` | `int` | unary `+` on `7`; converted to `unsigned` by the enclosing `+` |\n| `... / 8` | **`unsigned int`** ⇒ **unsigned division** | |\n| `18U + channels + (...)` | **`unsigned int`** | left-assoc, wrapping |\n\n**Conclusion: there is NO undefined behaviour.** Every arithmetic node has at least one `unsigned int` operand, so nothing is ever evaluated in `int`; all overflow is well-defined modular wraparound, and the `/8` is unsigned (no `INT_MIN/-1` and no signed-shift rounding). Divisor is the constant `8`, so no division by zero.\n\nThree independent confirmations:\n- **UBSan (primary evidence):** `clang -fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all` over `c_src/src/lib.c` on the full corner grid + 5M random triples → **zero reports**. `-fsanitize=integer` reports overflow at `lib.c:4:16, 4:27, 5:25, 5:36, 6:25, 6:55, 7:25, 7:37, 7:76` and **every one says \"unsigned int\"** — no `signed integer overflow` anywhere.\n- **Codegen:** `objdump` of the shipped C `.so` shows `shr $0x3` (logical, i.e. unsigned divide) and `add $0x7`, and GCC *reassociates* `T1+T2` into `bs*bd*((ch*(ch!=2))+(ch==2))` — a transform only legal for wrapping unsigned arithmetic.\n- The `+ +7` is a no-op unary plus (`add $0x7,%eax`).\n\n**Closed form (all mod 2^32):** `f = 18 + ch + ((bs·M + 7) udiv 8)` where `M = bd·ch·[ch≠2] + bd·[ch=2] + (bd + [bd≠32])·[ch=2]`.\n\n## 2. Rust faithfulness — **PASS**\n\n`src/lib.rs:45-76` is a node-for-node match: `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add` for every C `*`/`+`, `c_bool()` for each `int` 0/1 predicate, and plain `u32 / 8` (unsigned). Disassembly of the Rust cdylib is structurally identical to the C: `imul` (truncating 32×32→32), `shr $0x3`, `add $0x12`. **No input class where they can differ.** No divergent input found.\n\n**My own differential harness** (`$TMPDIR/audit/diff.c`, `exh.c`, `exh2.c`) dlopens both `.so`s, asserts `cfn != rfn`, and compares against a *third* independent `u64`-masked model I wrote from the C standard (deliberately not a copy of `src/lib.rs`):\n\n| sweep | count | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 11 × full 2^32 `bitdepth` sweeps (incl. `bs∈{1,3,8,65537,2^31,2^32-1}`, `ch∈{0,1,2,3,2^32-1}`) | 4.7e10 | 0 mismatch |\n| 7 × full 2^32 `blocksize` sweeps (incl. `(ch,bd)=(2,0)` ⇒ `M=1`, enumerating **every** 32-bit product `P`) | 3.0e10 | 0 mismatch |\n| 5 × full 2^32 `channels` sweeps (incl. `bs=0` ⇒ exhaustively validates `18+ch` for all 2^32 `ch`) | 2.1e10 | 0 mismatch |\n| 6 × **2-D exhaustive** `channels × bitdepth` over `0..65535` at `bs∈{0,1,4096,65537,2^31,2^32-1}` | 2.6e10 | 0 mismatch |\n| corner grid (113 values³, incl. products hitting exactly 2^32 and 2^32−1: `65537×65535`, `65536²`, `2×2^31`, `3×1431655765`, `17×252645135`, `257×16711935`), all `ch∈{0..9,2^31,2^32-1,2^32-2}` × `bd∈{0,31,32,33,2^32-1,2^32-2}`, all-zeros, all-0xFFFFFFFF | 1.7e6 | 0 mismatch |\n| 3,000,000,000 random triples (half with small `channels`) | 3.0e9 | 0 mismatch |\n\n**≈1.28 × 10^11 input triples, zero divergences**, and zero disagreements with my independent model.\n\n**Mutation-kill check on my own harness** (to prove it isn't vacuous): I built 6 deliberately-wrong `.so`s (signed `/8`, u64-no-wrap, `<32` instead of `!=32`, dropped `(ch!=2)` guard, `+8` instead of `+7`, saturating outer add) — my grid caught **all 6** within the first 200k comparisons.\n\n## 3. Untranslated code — **none**\n\n`readelf --dyn-syms c_src/build/libtranslated_rust.so`: exactly one defined global — `max_size_frame` (107 bytes). Undefined = 4 weak CRT hooks (`_ITM_*`, `__gmon_start__`, `__cxa_finalize@GLIBC_2.2.5`). Rust cdylib defines exactly one global too: `max_size_frame`. The only C/H files anywhere in the repo are `c_src/src/lib.c` and `c_src/include/lib.h`; `c_src/CMakeLists.txt:5-6` compiles only `src/lib.c`, with no `option()`/`add_definitions`/conditional sources and no `#if` in the source. `SYMBOLS.md` is accurate (including the quoted `nm` address `0000000000011dc0` for `target/debug`, which I reproduced exactly).\n\n## 4. Test review\n\n**The harness is genuine, not vacuous.** `tests/common/mod.rs:254-289` `dlopen`s both files and `dlsym`s `max_size_frame` from each; `tests/common/mod.rs:298-310` compares two distinct calls. Both profiles pass (27+21+4 tests, dev and release). I proved non-vacuity empirically by copying the repo to `$TMPDIR` and mutating the copy's `src/lib.rs`: **signed `/8`, `<32`, `>2`, `18→19`, `+7→+8`, saturating outer add, and u64-divide were all killed**; a `panic!` mutant aborted the run. (A `u64`-sum-then-truncate mutant survived — correctly, it is semantically equivalent.)\n\nFindings, most important first:\n\n1. **No freshness guard on the C ground truth — demonstrated silent vacuous pass.** `tests/common/mod.rs:57-98` returns any pre-existing `.so` in `c_src/build` without comparing its mtime to `c_src/src/lib.c`, while the Rust side *does* get `assert_fresher_than_sources` (`tests/common/mod.rs:186-204`). In my copy I changed `18U`→`19U` in `c_src/src/lib.c` and did **not** rebuild: all 52 tests still reported `ok`. (In the real tree the C `.so` *is* fresh — `lib.c` 13:16:13, `.so` 13:17:02 — so this is a latent robustness hole, not a current false pass.)\n\n2. **`find_so` picks the alphabetically-first `.so`, unpinned** (`tests/common/mod.rs:237-252`). I dropped `libaaa_decoy.so` into the copy's `c_src/build` and the harness silently adopted it as \"the C library\" (`C .so: .../libaaa_decoy.so`). It failed loudly only because my decoy was wrong; a stray artifact that happened to agree with Rust would pass vacuously. `run_all_tests.sh:18` uses an *unsorted* `find | head -1`, so the script and the harness can disagree about which `.so` is ground truth.\n\n3. **`differential.rs:290-303` (CONFIGS.md row 21, axis X8) contains a 400,000-iteration loop that asserts nothing in practice.** `P(sum ≥ 2^32−7) ≈ 1.6e-9`, so it finds 0 cases; I ran it with `--nocapture` and it prints `row21: 0 randomly-found numerator-wrap cases verified`. The row is *not* vacuous overall (`differential.rs:278-286` engineers the wrap deterministically), but that loop is pure cost. Same pattern is fine in `error_paths.rs:211-218`.\n\n4. **The claimed 2^32 exhaustive sweeps are never executed by the documented pipeline.** All four tests in `tests/exhaustive_axis.rs` are `#[ignore]` (lines 34, 74, 114, 152) and `run_all_tests.sh:64-77` never passes `--ignored`. I ran `exhaustive_multiplier_stereo` myself: it works and takes 27.2 s (all 20 sweeps ≈ 9 min). Also `exhaustive_multiplier_stereo` (line 153) is 100 % redundant with the `(1, 2)` pair inside `exhaustive_all_bitdepths` (line 81).\n\n5. **The \"independent oracle\" is not independent.** `tests/common/mod.rs:329-344` `oracle()` is textually the same expression tree as `src/lib.rs:52-75`, so a misreading of the C would be replicated in both. (I closed this gap with my own `u64`-masked model: 3.0e9 agreements.)\n\n6. **`ERRORS.md` claims that overstate the assertions:**\n   - `ERRORS.md:108` points at `VERIFICATION.md`, which **does not exist** in the repo.\n   - Row 19 (`ERRORS.md:70`) says \"Accepted for **all 2^32 patterns per argument**\" and `ERRORS.md:42` says \"the full 32-bit range is **swept randomly**\". The actual test `error_paths.rs:304-334` uses a fixed 76-element pattern list (76³ ≈ 4.4e5 calls) with no randomness. The claim is not supported by that row's assertions.\n   - `ERRORS.md:9-19` grep evidence — I re-ran all three greps and they reproduce exactly (exit 1 / lib.c:4 / lines 5-7 only). Accurate.\n   - Rows 1-18, 20, 21 each map to a real `#[test]` with real assertions; row 4's `20 + (bs+7)/8` and row 13's `f(1,2,0xFFFFFFFF)==20` are correct.\n\n7. **`CONFIGS.md` minor inaccuracies:** line 65 says seed `0x5EED_C0FFEE`, actual is `0x5EED_C0FF_EE00_0000` (`tests/common/mod.rs:366`). Rows 1-20 and 22-26 are all backed by real assertions; row 21 is the weak one (finding 3). `error_paths.rs:186` lists `0x1_0000_0000u64 as u32`, which is just `0` — mislabelled as an \"exactly-overflowing product\".\n\n8. **`symbol_parity.rs:70-76`** `is_runtime_glue` filters any name starting with `__`, which would hide a real C API symbol in the implementation-reserved namespace. Mitigated here by the `c_api.len() == 1` assertion at lines 108-112.\n\n9. `Cargo.toml:12` `panic = \"abort\"`: a Rust panic aborts the whole test binary rather than failing one test, so per-test attribution is lost (the run still fails — I confirmed with the panic mutant).\n\n## Gaps I would still want closed\n\n- **Assert the C `.so` is newer than `c_src/src/lib.c`** and **pin the C library filename** (`libtranslated_rust.so`, from the CMake project name) instead of `sorted().first()`.\n- Un-`#[ignore]` (or wire `--ignored` into `run_all_tests.sh`) the `exhaustive_axis` sweeps; ~9 min buys genuinely exhaustive coverage of `(P+7)/8` over all 2^32 `P`, of `18+ch` over all 2^32 `ch`, and of stereo `M` over all 2^32 `bd`.\n- **Residual unverifiable surface:** the only part of the 2^96 space not exhaustively covered by any test (mine included) is the truncating 32×32→32 multiply for *both* operands simultaneously large (`ch·bd` and `bs·M`, 2^64 pairs each). A needle mutant (`if bs==0xDEADBEEF && ch==0x12345678 && bd==0x0BADF00D { return 0 }`) survives the entire existing suite — I verified this. Only an SMT/bitvector equivalence proof would close it; no solver (z3/cvc5) is installed and there's no network. Structural argument in lieu: both sides emit the same `imul`/`shr` sequence over `Z/2^32`.\n- Replace `oracle()` with a derivation written from `lib.c` by a different route (e.g. `u64`-masked, as I did) so C+Rust can't share a misreading.\n\n**Nothing was modified.** `md5sum` of `src/lib.rs`, `tests/**`, `*.md`, `c_src/**` are unchanged (mtimes identical